WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.760
Silent movie references? Political satire?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.880
Conveniently timed lightning strikes?&nbsp;
Here are a few things about the 1985&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.840
sci-fi classic "Back to the Future" that you'll&nbsp;
only notice after watching it multiple times.
 
 

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.560
A lot of the foreshadowing in "Back&nbsp;
to the Future" isn't exactly subtle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.960
but some bits are easier to miss than&nbsp;
others. For example, when Marty McFly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.680
worries about sending out his audition tape&nbsp;
in the film's opening moments, he speculates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.800
about the record company telling him he has&nbsp;
"no future." Sure enough, he almost doesn't.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.840
If you look at some of these early moments long&nbsp;
enough, you find out how layered they really are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.280
especially once you know the course&nbsp;
that the movie is going to take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.440
In the scene where Principal Strickland&nbsp;
scolds Marty for being a slacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.880
it's easy to notice that he says that "history's&nbsp;
gonna change," but he also identifies George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.400
McFly's lack of drive — or lack of backbone,&nbsp;
really — as the reason Marty's father "never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.000
amounted to anything." Sure enough, that turns out&nbsp;
to be the exact part of history that changes.
 
 

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.200
It's always been obvious that the last few&nbsp;
minutes of "Back to the Future" serve to set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.640
up the plot of "Part II," but there's one bit of&nbsp;
sequel bait that only gets a double-take once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.040
already know what'll happen when Marty&nbsp;
gets to 2015. Right before the Libyan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.080
nationalists crash the party, Doc Brown&nbsp;
mentions his high-minded time travel goals:

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.560
"I've always dreamed of seeing the future, looking&nbsp;
beyond my years, seeing the progress of mankind."

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.680
Very noble and inspiring indeed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.400
But then he adds something that most of us&nbsp;
would probably find even more inspiring:

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.120
"I'll also be able to see who&nbsp;
wins the next 25 World Series."

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.480
It's a fine joke, but it's a lot more&nbsp;
foreboding in the context of Biff's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.760
use of the sports almanac a film later.&nbsp;
Plus, it makes Doc's outrage about Marty's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.040
identical idea for a money-making scheme in&nbsp;
"Part II" kind of hypocritical. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.200
the Doc who gleefully hoped to learn the&nbsp;
World Series winners isn't really the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.760
Doc who scolded Marty for besmirching&nbsp;
the nobility of time travel. Maybe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.920
extra 30 years he had to speculate on probable&nbsp;
consequences were able to change his mind.
 
 

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.200
If you're a fan of silent comedies, few&nbsp;
images are as iconic as poor Harold Lloyd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.240
dangling from the hand of an enormous&nbsp;
clock in 1923's "Safety Last."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.880
If you know the film — or even just the shot&nbsp;
— it's impossible not to see the resemblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.560
when Doc Brown finds himself hanging for dear&nbsp;
life from the hour hand. It's a great homage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.920
especially when you remember that Doc is played by&nbsp;
yet another, albeit unrelated Lloyd — Christopher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.440
this time. Lloyds of the world should probably&nbsp;
all stay away from clock towers, just in case.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.520
"Back to the Future" isn't content with&nbsp;
just one reference to "Safety Last," though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.560
it sneaks in a second — one that doubles as&nbsp;
foreshadowing for Doc's later bit of aerial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.360
comedy. In the film's opening, as the&nbsp;
camera pans over Doc's clock collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.480
we see that one of the clocks has a tiny figurine&nbsp;
of Harold Lloyd attached to one of the hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.360
We take it that Doc Brown is a&nbsp;
fan of "Safety Last" himself.
 
 

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.040
No one turns on a classic time travel&nbsp;
caper to see a small town mayoral race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.240
but you still shouldn't miss this cynical&nbsp;
joke about the cyclical nature of politics.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.920
In Marty's original 1985 timeline, mayor&nbsp;
Goldie Wilson is running for reelection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.040
and the megaphones installed on his campaign&nbsp;
van make it clear that "progress is his middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.880
name." Political and civic progress, that is,&nbsp;
because the slogan certainly isn't referring to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:36.800
creative progress. Back in 1955, Mayor Red Thomas&nbsp;
has his campaign car blare the exact same slogan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.200
His predecessor almost certainly&nbsp;
followed exactly the same script.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.960
If you're paying enough attention, you&nbsp;
might notice that the two campaigns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:50.400
match each other word for word, but subsequent&nbsp;
viewings give you more time to appreciate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.680
layered ironies at play. Another favorite:&nbsp;
both mayoral candidates are incumbents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.800
Their campaigns are promoting change… as the&nbsp;
reason to keep on re-electing the same guy.
 
 

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.680
There's been plenty of nostalgia for&nbsp;
the '80s over the past decade — just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.840
as the '70s and '80s had plenty&nbsp;
of similar nostalgia for the '50s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.960
"Back to the Future" was part of that wistful&nbsp;
cultural wave, and the set design proves it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.080
once upon a time, Hill Valley had&nbsp;
more civic pride and less vandalism.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
The movie's view of this nostalgia is nuanced,&nbsp;
however, and it doesn't hesitate to skewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.880
exactly this kind of longing for the past. In&nbsp;
"Back to the Future," we see that the '50s were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:37.200
far from innocent: after all, both Goldie Wilson&nbsp;
and the band playing the school dance get racist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:43.440
slurs thrown at them just for existing. The&nbsp;
Hill Valley of 1955 may not have had an adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.680
movie theater in the town square, but it still had&nbsp;
rapists and peeping toms. Compared with all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.880
Marty's fantasy of a night away at the lake with&nbsp;
his steady girlfriend seems positively wholesome.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:01.200
Even the most basic bits of nostalgia get subtly&nbsp;
taken to task here. Idealized family dinners?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.160
As soon as they get the chance,&nbsp;
Lorraine's family is thrilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.760
to watch TV while they're eating ...&nbsp;
just like the McFlys in the '80s.
 
 

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.560
Life imitates art — so when an unrecognizable&nbsp;
vessel crashes into your barn and a mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.400
figure in a bizarre full-body suit emerges,&nbsp;
you might well think aliens are responsible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.040
Especially if it's 1955 and you're a&nbsp;
kid with a collection of comic books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.560
Books, movies, TV, comics — these things are just&nbsp;
part of how we relate to the world around us.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.840
"Back to the Future" knows this, and leans into&nbsp;
it. One of Biff's pals has the gimmick of always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.360
wearing a pair of red and blue 3-D glasses&nbsp;
around, a nod to the '50s craze for 3-D…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:48.000
which would eventually return in Marty's '80s, and&nbsp;
again without glasses in the futuristic 2015. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.040
just one of several ways the film shows that the&nbsp;
more things change, the more they stay the same.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.560
However, the best bit of meta-fictional overlap&nbsp;
in "Back to the Future" comes when Marty hooks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.560
up his camcorder to Doc's 1955 TV to show&nbsp;
him the footage taken right before Marty's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.680
desperate escape through time. As Marty and&nbsp;
Doc rewind and fast-forward to get to the parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.240
they need in the tape, they're duplicating&nbsp;
the time-travel process in their own way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.000
moving backwards and forwards through&nbsp;
Marty's future historical record.
 
 

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.120
Throughout "Back to the Future," Marty&nbsp;
always seems to have the same problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.200
he's constantly late. Of course, it&nbsp;
doesn't help that all the clocks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.280
Doc's workshop are set to the wrong time&nbsp;
— but those clocks have nothing to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.440
him lingering at the dance when he knows he&nbsp;
has to make it to the clock tower on time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.240
Priorities, Marty! We all like "Johnny B.&nbsp;
Goode," but you don't want to get stuck in 1955!

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.040
Of course, the ongoing detail of&nbsp;
Marty's lateness gets a satisfying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.320
and briefly bittersweet twist: when Marty&nbsp;
finally thinks to arrive somewhere early,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.680
it's so he can save Doc's life. Marty giving&nbsp;
himself 10 additional minutes to get to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.240
mall parking lot is an excellent end to his&nbsp;
character arc in the first film. He may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.520
late for everything else — even his own future&nbsp;
— but when his best friend's life is at stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.960
he's able to step up and work&nbsp;
past his most unfortunate habit.
 
 

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.760
When it comes to time, nobody has any business&nbsp;
criticizing Dr. Emmett Brown. He invented the flux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.640
capacitor and conquered the laws of physics while&nbsp;
everyone else is still frustrated about Daylight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.080
Savings Time. However, there's one specific&nbsp;
detail that it doesn't take a doctorate to notice.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.800
Thanks to the "Save the Clock Tower"&nbsp;
campaign, Marty knows the exact time and date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.240
that the Hill Valley landmark will be struck&nbsp;
by lightning… sort of. He knows the day, hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.520
and minute, but most lightning strikes are over&nbsp;
the same second they began. Marty has to hit Doc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.960
Brown's wire at the exact right moment, and&nbsp;
he has no idea which moment that might be.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.440
As Doc pointed out earlier, there are a lot of&nbsp;
fiddly little details that have to be worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.240
out exactly right for Marty to be able to&nbsp;
make it back to 1985 — but this one seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.720
more impossible than the rest. It's a good&nbsp;
thing that once Doc comes back from the far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.440
off year of 2015, he's got a more&nbsp;
reliable, trash-based fuel source.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.600
At first glance, Marty McFly and his dad&nbsp;
don't seem to have much in common. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:17.200
encountering them both in 1955, even Doc Brown&nbsp;
can't quite believe that the two are related.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.280
"Maybe you were adopted."

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:24.000
That's not true, of course, considering that&nbsp;
the whole plot of the film centers around Marty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.520
being George and Lorraine's biological son.&nbsp;
It's concrete proof of the McFly bloodline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.040
but it's not exactly necessary. Against all odds,&nbsp;
the film makes it clear that George and Marty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.480
actually have quite a lot in common, right down&nbsp;
to their body language. Even more meaningfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:45.440
the two share some identical anxieties about&nbsp;
rejection: neither one of them can initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.560
work up the courage to submit their creative work&nbsp;
to a publisher or label that might turn it down.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.840
Thankfully, all the father-son parallels get&nbsp;
a heartwarming payoff in the film's final act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.480
Marty helps George find his courage and win&nbsp;
over Lorraine — and seeing his father stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.200
up for himself gives him the motivation he needs&nbsp;
to take over for Marvin Berry and lose himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.000
in the music. And when Marty gets gawked at by a&nbsp;
bunch of '50s teens and teachers who can't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.480
what they're hearing? It turns out that he can&nbsp;
take rejection pretty gracefully, after all.
 
 

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.880
Kids and their parents have always clashed over&nbsp;
music: The art form might change over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.200
but once our tastes are fixed, they&nbsp;
often stay the same. Sooner or later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.560
we're all bound to get confused by the latest&nbsp;
hits. Needless to say, Marty experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.640
this phenomenon firsthand when he plays an&nbsp;
'80s-style guitar solo for a baffled '50s crowd.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.240
"I guess you guys aren't ready for that&nbsp;
yet… but your kids are gonna love it."

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.120
That's not the only time "Back to the Future"&nbsp;
shows older generations suffering when their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:52.480
kids crank up the volume on the newest hits.&nbsp;
When Marty dons his hazmat suit to scare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:58.960
1955 version of his dad into asking out Lorraine,&nbsp;
he amps up the weirdness factor by blasting George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.320
with a few seconds of Eddie Van Halen cranked&nbsp;
up to 10. George writhes in agony as he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.960
literally tortured by Marty's taste in music:&nbsp;
he doesn't get it, but his son sure does.
 
 

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.680
George McFly's burgeoning career as a novelist&nbsp;
is the perfect cherry on top of his happier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.040
better-adjusted life in the altered 1985. If&nbsp;
you take a good look at the cover of "A Match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.680
Made in Space," you might notice that it bears&nbsp;
a resemblance to those of real-life science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.360
fiction author Robert A. Heinlein. Connections&nbsp;
between George and Heinlein are likely anyway:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:35.280
He's one of the authors George would have&nbsp;
absolutely read growing up, considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.400
that the teenage George was ready to skip the big&nbsp;
dance so he could watch "Science Fiction Theater."

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:44.800
Although George doesn't know it, his own life&nbsp;
winds up being like something out of a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.280
Heinlein story… and not just because he's&nbsp;
living in a science fiction movie himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.320
Heinlein's short story "All You Zombies"&nbsp;
deals with how time travel paradoxes could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:00.000
lead to a person becoming several of their own&nbsp;
ancestors, for example. In a decidedly less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.880
family-friendly version of "Back to the Future,"&nbsp;
we could see that happening to Marty, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:08.640
That's not the only Heinlein story&nbsp;
that echoes Marty's journey, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:14.080
Heinlein's novel "Time Enough for Love" tackles&nbsp;
multiple kinds of incest in a science fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:20.800
setting, including a romance between a mother and&nbsp;
her son. Awkward doesn't even begin to cover it.

